{
  "description": "Issue #962, execution twin of `heritage/check/heritage-value-of-key-reader-across-a-newline`. The check twin proves the newline spellings are not DIAGNOSED; this one proves they MEAN what their one-line spellings mean, which no diagnostic can show. The dict rows are why it exists: before the fix `print { a: value of :d` + newline + `for key :k }` raised two `ol-bad-token` describing an incomplete reader while ALSO producing a dict that held one entry keyed `key` -- whose value was a call to a procedure `k` -- with the entry `a` gone. Nothing reported that second half, so a fix that merely silenced the diagnostics would have passed the check twin with the wrong tree still in place. Every spelling below is followed by its one-line twin, with no unpaired rows, and the two `print` payloads of a pair are identical; that identity is the runtime witness of the equivalence, while the exact tree is asserted in `packages/parser/src/value-of-key-newline.test.mjs`, which compares the two ASTs directly. The `instruction` spans deliberately differ within a pair -- the newline spelling really does span two source lines -- so what is asserted here is identity of the VALUES, not of the spans. This is also where statement segmentation is genuinely pinned: the event stream carries one `instruction` per statement, so a reading that merged the closing `print 1` / `print 2` would drop an event, which a diagnostics-only fixture could never notice. `execute()` runs `parse()` and never `check()`, so the runtime holds its own copy of these rules. The uppercase and mixed-case pairs carry the same weight as the rest: keywords are case-insensitive (`spec/grammar.md:13`), so `FOR KEY` across a newline must not merely parse but MEAN what its lowercase one-line twin means, which is what the identical print payloads assert. `style` is a check-only flag that `execute()` never reads, so it has no bearing here either way; the tail words `of`/`for`/`key` are not linted at all in any case. The reader operates on a dict, so Heritage also needs Data (`spec/conformance.md:305`).",
